Perfect — here’s a compact, copy-pasteable kit that covers all three:
	•	fit a tail-aware vine (with asymmetric tails),
	•	sample back to time series shocks for your AR+GARCH reconstruction,
	•	test tail dependence to see if the model captures crashes/rallies.

I’ll assume you already have standardized residuals Z (shape T×d, e.g., d=5 for [PC1..PC4, dlogS]) and (optionally) AR means mu_t and GARCH scales sigma_t for each series.

⸻

1) Fit a vine copula (tail-aware)

# pip install pyvinecopulib scipy numpy
import numpy as np, pyvinecopulib as pv
from scipy import stats

# --- (A) Optional: residualize PCs on dlogS to remove near-linearity ---
# If PCj ~ a + b * dlogS with |r| ~ 1, residualize; otherwise skip this block.
def residualize_on_factor(Z, factor_col=-1):
    Z = np.asarray(Z); d = Z.shape[1]
    X = Z[:, factor_col]
    Zout = Z.copy()
    Xc = (X - X.mean())  # no intercept -> OK since Z are standardized
    denom = (Xc @ Xc)
    for j in range(d):
        if j == factor_col: continue
        beta = (Xc @ Z[:, j]) / (denom + 1e-12)
        Zout[:, j] = Z[:, j] - beta * Xc
        # re-standardize to var=1
        Zout[:, j] = (Zout[:, j] - Zout[:, j].mean()) / Zout[:, j].std(ddof=1)
    return Zout

# Z_res = residualize_on_factor(Z, factor_col=4)  # if column 4 is dlogS
Z_res = Z  # or keep as-is if not needed

# --- (B) Convert to pseudo-observations (ranks) ---
def pseudo_obs(col):
    r = stats.rankdata(col, method="average")
    return r / (len(col) + 1.0)
U = np.column_stack([pseudo_obs(Z_res[:, j]) for j in range(Z_res.shape[1])])

# --- (C) Fit a tail-aware R-vine ---
controls = pv.FitControlsVinecop(
    family_set=[
        pv.BicopFamily.student,   # symmetric tail dep
        pv.BicopFamily.clayton,   # lower-tail dep
        pv.BicopFamily.gumbel,    # upper-tail dep
        pv.BicopFamily.gaussian,  # no tail dep
        pv.BicopFamily.frank      # no tail dep (center)
    ],
    rotations=True,                       # allow rotated tails (for negative/asymmetric dep)
    selection_criterion=pv.FitMethod.aic, # AIC or BIC; AIC often captures tails better
    parametric_method=pv.ParametricMethod.mle
)
vine = pv.Vinecop(U, controls)
print(vine)  # shows structure + chosen families per edge

Notes
	•	This learns both the tree structure and the pair-copula family per edge (e.g., Clayton around ΔlogS if lower-tail clustering).
	•	If the fit keeps picking Gaussian everywhere, temporarily restrict family_set to [student, clayton, gumbel], fit, then re-enable Gaussian/Frank.

