3) Check tail dependence (does it capture tails?)

We’ll compare empirical vs model lower/upper joint tail probabilities for selected pairs (e.g., PC1 vs dlogS). Optionally, add a Rosenblatt PIT check.

# --- (A) Co-exceedance stats at several tail levels ---
def tail_coexceedance_table(U, model, q_list=(0.05, 0.02, 0.01), pairs=None,
                            nsim=200_000, seed=42):
    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)
    d = U.shape[1]
    if pairs is None:
        pairs = [(i, j) for i in range(d) for j in range(i+1, d)]
    Usim = model.simulate(nsim, seeds=[int(rng.integers(1e9))])
    rows = []
    for (i, j) in pairs:
        for q in q_list:
            empL = np.mean((U[:, i] < q) & (U[:, j] < q))
            empU = np.mean((U[:, i] > 1 - q) & (U[:, j] > 1 - q))
            modL = np.mean((Usim[:, i] < q) & (Usim[:, j] < q))
            modU = np.mean((Usim[:, i] > 1 - q) & (Usim[:, j] > 1 - q))
            lamL = empL / q if q > 0 else np.nan
            lamU = empU / q if q > 0 else np.nan
            lamL_m = modL / q if q > 0 else np.nan
            lamU_m = modU / q if q > 0 else np.nan
            rows.append({
                "pair": f"{i}-{j}", "q": q,
                "emp_lower": empL, "mod_lower": modL, "lambdaL_emp": lamL, "lambdaL_mod": lamL_m,
                "emp_upper": empU, "mod_upper": modU, "lambdaU_emp": lamU, "lambdaU_mod": lamU_m
            })
    return rows

U = np.column_stack([pseudo_obs(Z_res[:, j]) for j in range(Z_res.shape[1])])
tail_stats = tail_coexceedance_table(U, vine, q_list=(0.05,0.02,0.01), pairs=[(0,4),(1,4),(2,4),(3,4)])
for r in tail_stats:
    print(r)

Interpretation
	•	For a good fit, mod_lower should be close to emp_lower (especially for pairs with ΔlogS).
	•	If mod_lower << emp_lower → the model underestimates joint crashes: allow Clayton (and rotations) or use t with smaller ν.
	•	If lower tails are good but upper tails off → ensure Gumbel is allowed (and rotated if signs flip).

# --- (B) (Optional) Rosenblatt transform check ---
# For a vine, use the model’s cdf to transform U→R where R should be i.i.d. Uniform(0,1).
# pyvinecopulib exposes PIT via h-functions; a simple proxy is to check PITs of pairwise conditional cdfs or run a pseudo-likelihood PIT.
# Quick practical alternative: compare Kendall’s tau and tail co-exceedances across real vs simulated U; if both match, you’re usually fine.


⸻

Quick “defaults” that work well
	•	Margins: always use pseudo-observations + empirical inverse CDF when mapping back.
	•	Families: start with {student, clayton, gumbel} + rotations=True. Add Gaussian/Frank later if AIC insists.
	•	Regimes: if tails strengthen in high VIX, fit two vines and simulate conditional on regime.
	•	Near-collinearity: residualize PCs on ΔlogS (or make ΔlogS the hub in a star-vine).
	•	Reconstruction: x_t = μ_t + σ_t * z_t (from your AR+GARCH); then rebuild the vol surface with today’s PCA basis; enforce no-arb before pricing.

